notes.body:
Arrays and structs let you group data: an array holds many values of the same type, and a struct holds several named values that may have different types.

One-dimensional arrays
    int v[5] = {1, 2, 3};    /* v[3] and v[4] are set to 0 */
- Indices run from 0 to N - 1. C does not check bounds: writing v[5] is undefined behaviour and may silently corrupt other variables.
- The number of elements is sizeof v / sizeof v[0], but only where v is the array itself. Inside a function that receives the array, the parameter is just a pointer, so pass the length separately.
- When passed to a function, an array is not copied: the function gets the address of the first element and can change the caller's elements.

Multi-dimensional arrays
    int m[2][3] = { {1, 2, 3}, {4, 5, 6} };
- m has 2 rows of 3 columns; m[1][2] is 6.
- C stores arrays in row-major order: the whole of row 0, then row 1. Element m[i][j] of an array with C columns is i × C + j elements from the start, so in this array m[1][1] (value 5) is 4 elements along.
- To pass m to a function, every dimension except the first must be given: void print(int m[][3], int rows);
- Nested loops are the normal way to process them; summing m above with two for loops gives 21.

Strings
- A string is a char array ending in the null character '\0'.
- char s[] = "EEE"; has sizeof s equal to 4 (three letters plus '\0'), while strlen(s) from <string.h> returns 3.
- Use strcpy, strcmp and strlen from <string.h>; == on two strings compares addresses, not contents.

Structs
    struct reading {
        int id;
        double value;
    };
    struct reading r = {1, 2.5};
    r.value = 3.0;
- Members are accessed with the dot operator.
- With a pointer to a struct, use the arrow: p->value means (*p).value.
- typedef struct { ... } Reading; lets you write Reading instead of struct reading.
- Assignment copies every member (r2 = r1;), but you cannot compare structs with ==.
- The compiler may insert padding for alignment, so sizeof a struct can exceed the sum of its members: a struct holding a char and an int is 8 bytes on a typical PC, not 5.

Arrays of structs
A tested function that averages an array of readings:
    double mean_value(const struct reading r[], int n)
    {
        double total = 0.0;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
            total += r[i].value;
        return total / n;
    }
With values 2.5, 3.0 and 4.0 it returns 3.1666..., printed as 3.17 with %.2f. const promises the function will not change the array.

Exam tips: count the '\0' when sizing strings, use i × columns + j for row-major offsets, and remember that a function cannot find an array's length from its parameter.

Control flow decides which statements run and how often. You need to choose the right construct for each job and trace loops exactly, because "how many times does this run?" and "what is printed?" are standard exam questions.

Conditions
- Relational operators: <, <=, >, >=, == (equal) and != (not equal).
- Logical operators: && (and), || (or), ! (not). They short-circuit: in (p != NULL && p->x > 0) the second test is skipped when p is NULL, which is exactly what makes it safe.
- C treats 0 as false and any non-zero value as true.
- A classic bug is writing if (x = 5), which assigns 5 and is always true; you meant if (x == 5). -Wall warns about this.

if, else if, else
    if (temp > 80)
        fan = FULL;
    else if (temp > 50)
        fan = HALF;
    else
        fan = OFF;
Only the first true branch runs. Use braces whenever a branch has more than one statement.

switch
    switch (mode) {
    case 1:  start(); break;
    case 2:  stop();  break;
    default: report_error();
    }
- The expression must be an integer type (char counts) and case labels must be constants.
- Execution jumps to the matching case and carries on into the following cases until it hits break. This "fall-through" is occasionally useful but is usually a bug, and gcc's -Wextra warns about it.
- default handles every value not listed.

Loops
- while (condition) { ... } tests first, so the body may run zero times.
- do { ... } while (condition); tests at the end, so the body always runs at least once. It suits input validation: keep asking until the value is acceptable.
- for (init; condition; update) { ... } suits counting loops and keeps the loop variable's set-up, test and update in one line.
- break leaves the innermost loop or switch immediately; continue skips to the next iteration.
- while (1) { ... } is an intentional infinite loop, the standard structure of a microcontroller program.

Counting iterations
- for (i = 0; i < n; i++) runs n times; for (i = 0; i <= n; i++) runs n + 1 times. Off-by-one errors are the most common loop bug.
- For a stepped loop, list the values: for (i = 0; i <= 10; i += 2) takes i = 0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, so it runs 6 times; the test fails at i = 12.
- Starting at i = 10, while (i < 5) i++; leaves i at 10, but do { i++; } while (i < 5); leaves it at 11, because the body runs once before the test.

Nested loops
    for (int r = 1; r <= 3; r++) {
        for (int k = 0; k < r; k++)
            printf("*");
        printf("\n");
    }
prints a triangle of 1, 2 and 3 stars. The inner loop restarts from zero on every pass of the outer loop.

The conditional operator
max = (a > b) ? a : b; is a compact if-else that produces a value.

Exam tips: when tracing, write down the loop variable's value at every test, not just at every pass through the body, and check every switch case for a missing break.

Functions break a program into named, testable pieces. A good function does one job, takes what it needs as parameters and gives back a result.

Anatomy of a function
    int max_of(const int a[], int n)    /* return type, name, parameters */
    {
        int best = a[0];
        for (int i = 1; i < n; i++)
            if (a[i] > best)
                best = a[i];
        return best;
    }
Called as max_of(data, 4) with data = {3, 9, -2, 7}, it returns 9.
- void as the return type means nothing is returned; (void) as the parameter list means no parameters.
- return ends the function immediately and hands back the value.

Declarations and definitions
- A definition contains the body. A declaration, or prototype, gives only the signature and ends with a semicolon: int max_of(const int a[], int n);
- The compiler must see a declaration before a call so it can check the arguments. Put prototypes above main, or in a header file (.h) that you #include, and the definitions in a .c file.

Parameters are passed by value
- Each parameter is a new local variable initialised with a copy of the argument. Changing it inside the function does not affect the caller's variable.
- To let a function change a caller's variable, or to return more than one result, pass a pointer (see the pointers topic) or return a struct.
- Arrays look like an exception: what is copied is the address of the first element, so the function can change the caller's elements. Mark read-only array parameters const.

Scope and lifetime
- Local (automatic) variables exist only while their function is running and are created afresh on each call, usually on the stack.
- A static local variable is created once and keeps its value between calls:
    int next(void)
    {
        static int c = 0;   /* initialised only once */
        c++;
        return c;
    }
  Calling next() three times returns 1, 2 and then 3.
- Global variables, declared outside all functions, are visible everywhere in the file. They make code harder to test, so use them sparingly; in embedded code they are often used to share data with interrupt routines.

Recursion
A function may call itself if it has a base case that stops the recursion:
    int f(int n)
    {
        if (n <= 1)
            return 1;          /* base case */
        return n * f(n - 1);   /* recursive case */
    }
f(4) = 4 × 3 × 2 × 1 = 24 and f(5) = 120. Each call gets its own stack frame, so very deep recursion can overflow the stack, a real risk on a microcontroller with little RAM.

Good practice
- Choose descriptive names and keep functions short.
- Validate inputs (for example n >= 1) or state the assumption in a comment.
- Test each function on its own with known inputs before combining them.

Exam tips: trace recursive calls as a stack written out by hand, and remember that static changes the lifetime of a local variable, not whether it can be modified.

Ordinary local variables live on the stack and disappear when their function returns, and their sizes must usually be known when you write the code. Dynamic allocation on the heap gives you memory whose size is chosen while the program runs and which lasts until you release it.

The functions (all in <stdlib.h>)
- malloc(bytes) returns a void * to a block of at least that many bytes, with indeterminate contents, or NULL if it fails.
- calloc(count, size) allocates count × size bytes and sets them all to zero.
- realloc(ptr, new_bytes) resizes a block, keeping the old contents; it may move the block and return a different address, or return NULL and leave the old block untouched.
- free(ptr) releases a block obtained from these functions. free(NULL) is allowed and does nothing.

The standard pattern
    int n = 5;
    int *v = malloc(n * sizeof *v);
    if (v == NULL) {
        fprintf(stderr, "out of memory\n");
        return 1;
    }
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
        v[i] = i * i;
- n * sizeof *v asks for n elements of whatever type v points to, so it stays right if you later change int to double. Asking for malloc(n) would give only n bytes.
- Once allocated, v is used exactly like an array.

Growing safely with realloc
    int *tmp = realloc(v, 2 * n * sizeof *v);
    if (tmp == NULL) {
        free(v);         /* old block is still valid: release it */
        return 1;
    }
    v = tmp;
Assigning straight back with v = realloc(v, ...) loses the only pointer to the old block if realloc fails. In the tested program, filling all 10 elements with squares gives a total of 285, and valgrind reports no errors or leaks.

Releasing memory
    free(v);
    v = NULL;
Setting the pointer to NULL afterwards turns an accidental later use into an obvious crash instead of silent corruption.

The classic errors
- Memory leak: losing the last pointer to a block without freeing it. Long-running programs, and microcontrollers, eventually run out of memory.
- Use after free (dangling pointer): reading or writing a block after free.
- Double free: calling free twice on the same block; undefined behaviour.
- Freeing memory that did not come from malloc, such as a local array.
- Returning a pointer to a local array: the array vanishes when the function returns. Return malloc'd memory instead and make it clear that the caller must free it.
- Not checking for NULL.

Tools
valgrind ./prog, or compiling with gcc -fsanitize=address -g, reports leaks, invalid reads and writes, and double frees. Use them on every assignment.

Embedded systems
Microcontrollers have kilobytes of RAM, and repeated malloc and free can fragment the heap until a request fails. Many embedded coding standards, such as MISRA C, forbid dynamic allocation, so firmware usually uses fixed-size arrays sized at compile time.

Exam tips: for each malloc, be able to say which line frees it, and always write the NULL check; examiners give marks for it.

Linked structures are built from nodes allocated with malloc and joined by pointers. They grow and shrink one node at a time, which arrays cannot do cheaply.

Singly linked list
    struct node {
        int data;
        struct node *next;   /* NULL marks the end */
    };
The list is reached through a head pointer, which is NULL for an empty list.

Insert at the front (constant time)
    int push_front(struct node **head, int value)
    {
        struct node *n = malloc(sizeof *n);
        if (n == NULL)
            return 0;
        n->data = value;
        n->next = *head;   /* new node points at old first node */
        *head = n;         /* head now points at new node */
        return 1;
    }
The function takes struct node ** because it must change the caller's head pointer; passing struct node * would change only a copy. Pushing 5, then 8, then 1 onto an empty list and printing from the head gives 1 8 5.

Traverse, remove, free
- Traverse: for (p = head; p != NULL; p = p->next) visit p->data;
- Remove a node: find it while remembering the previous node, set prev->next = victim->next (or move head if it is the first), then free(victim).
- Free a whole list by saving p->next before calling free(p), because after free you may not read p->next.

Arrays versus lists
- Array: any element reached directly by index, but inserting in the middle means shifting elements, and growing means realloc.
- List: inserting or removing at a known position just relinks pointers, but finding the k-th element means walking k nodes, so search is O(n). Each node also costs extra memory for its pointer.

Binary search trees (BSTs)
    struct tnode {
        int key;
        struct tnode *left, *right;
    };
The BST rule: every key in a node's left subtree is smaller than the node's key and every key in its right subtree is larger.

Insert recursively
    struct tnode *insert(struct tnode *root, int key)
    {
        if (root == NULL) {
            struct tnode *t = malloc(sizeof *t);
            if (t == NULL) exit(1);
            t->key = key;
            t->left = t->right = NULL;
            return t;
        }
        if (key < root->key)
            root->left = insert(root->left, key);
        else if (key > root->key)
            root->right = insert(root->right, key);
        return root;     /* duplicates ignored */
    }

Traversals (tested on keys inserted in the order 40, 20, 60, 10, 30, 50, 70)
- In-order (left, node, right): 10 20 30 40 50 60 70, always sorted.
- Pre-order (node, left, right): 40 20 10 30 60 50 70, useful for copying a tree.
- Post-order (left, right, node): 10 30 20 50 70 60 40, the order to free nodes, because children are released before their parent.

Efficiency
Search, insert and delete follow one path from the root, so they take time proportional to the height. A balanced tree of n nodes has height about log2 n; inserting keys in sorted order produces a chain with height n that is no better than a linked list (inserting 1 to 5 in order gives height 5, counting nodes on the longest root-to-leaf path; some books count edges instead, giving 4).

Deleting from a BST
- Leaf: free it and set the parent's pointer to NULL.
- One child: link the parent straight to that child.
- Two children: copy in the in-order successor (the smallest key in the right subtree), then delete that successor node.

Exam tips: draw the tree for any given insertion order before writing traversals, and remember that in-order output is sorted, which is a quick check on your answer.

