questions.modelAnswer:
void swap(int *a, int *b)
{
    int t = *a;
    *a = *b;
    *b = t;
}
Called as swap(&x, &y);. In C every argument is passed by value, so a function with int parameters gets copies of x and y; swapping the copies changes nothing in the caller and the copies vanish when the function returns. Passing the addresses means the function's parameters point at the caller's own variables, and assigning through *a and *b changes them.

