questions.modelAnswer:
(a)
typedef struct {
    int channel;
    double volts;
} Sample;

(b)
int stats(const Sample *s, int n, double *mean, double *max)
{
    if (n < 1)
        return 0;
    double total = 0.0;
    *max = s[0].volts;          /* not 0: all readings could be negative */
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        total += s[i].volts;
        if (s[i].volts > *max)
            *max = s[i].volts;
    }
    *mean = total / n;
    return 1;
}
C can return only one value, so the two results go back through pointers: the caller passes the addresses of its own variables, and the function writes through them.

(c)
int main(void)
{
    int n;
    if (scanf("%d", &n) != 1 || n < 1)
        return 1;

    Sample *s = malloc(n * sizeof *s);
    if (s == NULL)
        return 1;

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        s[i].channel = i;
        if (scanf("%lf", &s[i].volts) != 1) {
            free(s);            /* do not leak on the error path */
            return 1;
        }
    }

    double mean, max;
    if (stats(s, n, &mean, &max))
        printf("mean = %.3f V, max = %.3f V\n", mean, max);

    free(s);
    return 0;
}
(needs #include <stdio.h> and <stdlib.h>). Note %lf in scanf for a double, but %f in printf.

(d) The values sum to 1.2 + 3.3 + 0.5 + 2.0 = 7.0, so the mean is 7.0 / 4 = 1.75 and the maximum is 3.3. The program prints:
mean = 1.750 V, max = 3.300 V
This exact program was compiled with gcc -Wall -Wextra and run under valgrind on this input, which reported no memory errors or leaks.

(a)
struct tnode {
    int key;
    struct tnode *left, *right;
};

(b)
struct tnode *insert(struct tnode *root, int key)
{
    if (root == NULL) {
        struct tnode *t = malloc(sizeof *t);
        if (t == NULL)
            return NULL;            /* caller should check */
        t->key = key;
        t->left = t->right = NULL;
        return t;
    }
    if (key < root->key)
        root->left = insert(root->left, key);
    else if (key > root->key)
        root->right = insert(root->right, key);
    return root;                    /* equal keys: ignored */
}
Assigning the result back to root->left or root->right is what links a newly created node into the tree.

(c) 50 is the root. 30 goes left of 50 and 70 right. 20 goes left of 30, 40 right of 30. 60 goes left of 70, 80 right of 70. 35 is less than 50, greater than 30 and less than 40, so it becomes the left child of 40.
In-order: 20 30 35 40 50 60 70 80 (sorted, as it must be).
Pre-order: 50 30 20 40 35 70 60 80.
Post-order: 20 35 40 30 60 80 70 50.
Height: the longest path is 50, 30, 40, 35, so the height is 4.

(d) Searching for 35 compares with 50, 30, 40 and then 35: 4 comparisons. Searching for 65 compares with 50 (go right), 70 (go left) and 60 (go right, which is NULL): 3 comparisons, then it reports that 65 is absent.

(e)
void free_tree(struct tnode *t)
{
    if (t == NULL)
        return;
    free_tree(t->left);
    free_tree(t->right);
    free(t);
}
This is a post-order traversal. If a node were freed first, reading t->left and t->right afterwards would be use-after-free, and its subtrees would be leaked or corrupted.
If the keys arrive in ascending order, each new key goes to the right of the previous one, so the tree becomes a chain of height n, in effect a linked list. Search then needs up to n comparisons instead of about log2 n for a balanced tree.

All the code and traversals above were checked by compiling and running them (gcc -Wall -Wextra, valgrind reporting no leaks).
